{
  "section": "replication-consistency",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "replication-topologies",
      "title": "Leader-follower, multi-leader and leaderless replication",
      "hours": 4,
      "target": [
        "explain"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "leader-follower-replication",
        "multi-leader-replication-vocabulary",
        "leaderless-replication-vocabulary"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Trace a write and its acknowledgement through each of the three replication topologies.",
        "Choose write authority, read routing and conflict handling for a disconnected client.",
        "Explain why a coordinator in a leaderless store is not an ordering leader."
      ],
      "why": "Build on Replication, quorums and consensus vocabulary. The new job is to turn each topology into an operation path and identify exactly where writes can diverge.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "distributed-systems/replication-consistency/replication-acks-log-lag",
          "note": "Specify what each acknowledgement on the path means."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "ddia-replication-ds4",
          "title": "DDIA, 2nd edition: the three replication architectures",
          "url": "https://dataintensive.net/",
          "locator": "Chapter 6, Replication: Single-Leader Replication, pp. 198 to 200; Multi-Leader Replication through Sync Engines and Local-First Software, pp. 215 to 222; Leaderless Replication, pp. 229 to 230",
          "depth": "Review the single-leader path from Systems briefly. Read the multi-leader topology and local-first discussion closely; stop before Dealing with Conflicting Writes."
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "Separate write authority from network shape. Several shard leaders are not several leaders for the same key.",
        "An asynchronously connected multi-leader store can acknowledge conflicting writes independently. It needs both dissemination and a conflict policy.",
        "A leaderless coordinator gathers replica responses. That role alone does not impose a common write order.",
        "Loop suppression and dependency ordering are different jobs. Deduplicating a forwarded update does not ensure that its prerequisite has arrived."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Draw three local replicas and two clients, with a partition separating one replica from the others. For each topology, trace two writes to the same key and a subsequent read on each side. Mark who may acknowledge, which replicas hold each version, and which read outcomes depend on an as-yet-unspecified conflict or quorum rule. Do not give a topology a consistency label until those rules are stated.",
      "traps": [
        "Calling all-to-all forwarding leaderless when each node independently orders and accepts local writes as a leader.",
        "Assuming an extra replica improves write availability when the acknowledgement rule still waits for every replica.",
        "Treating a topology diagram as a durability or freshness contract."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Does a client contacting only one coordinator imply single-leader replication?",
          "a": "No. The coordinator may fan out to interchangeable replicas without assigning a common order. Write authority and ordering, not the client connection count, distinguish the designs."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can a disconnected multi-leader replica accept edits without proving that they preserve a global uniqueness constraint?",
          "a": "Yes, but accepting edits independently does not enforce unrestricted global uniqueness. The design needs coordination, preallocated disjoint authority, or an explicit conflict outcome."
        },
        {
          "q": "Why can an insert and a dependent update arrive in the wrong order in an all-to-all topology?",
          "a": "They can travel over different links with different delays. Redundant paths do not themselves carry or enforce the dependency."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [],
      "practiceNote": "The section mini-project covers this: configurable write authority and read routing in the local loglab replicated-store model, with topology-specific partition histories.",
      "stopWhen": "Your three path diagrams identify every acknowledgement and divergent version, and you can state which missing policy prevents predicting a read result.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "DDIA supplies the topology and dependency cases beyond the Systems introduction; the mini-project turns the paths into executable policies."
      }
    },
